              <div class="post-faq">
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Do I need a permit to remodel my kitchen in Media, PA?</h3>
                  <p>In most cases, yes, if the remodel involves relocating plumbing or electrical, removing or altering a wall, or changing the room's footprint. A simple cabinet and countertop swap with no plumbing or electrical changes typically does not require a permit, but the moment a sink, outlet, or wall moves, Media Borough's UCC-based permitting requirement applies.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How much does a home remodeling permit cost in Delaware County, PA?</h3>
                  <p>Costs vary by township and project valuation, but typical ranges run from around $150 to $500 for a kitchen or bathroom remodel permit, and several hundred to a few thousand dollars for a home addition.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How long does it take to get a building permit in Media, PA?</h3>
                  <p>Media Borough is generally on the faster end of Delaware County's 49 municipalities, often processing straightforward remodel permits in 1 to 2 weeks with complete drawings. Other townships can take 4 to 8 weeks, and structurally complex projects can take 3 to 6 weeks or longer.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What happens if I remodel without a permit in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>You risk a stop-work order and fines, having to obtain a retroactive permit, denied insurance claims, and complications during a future home sale if a buyer's inspection uncovers unpermitted work.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Does a bathroom remodel need a permit in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>Yes, if the project relocates a toilet, tub, or shower drain, adds a new fixture, or involves electrical work beyond a straightforward like-for-like fixture swap. A cosmetic bathroom refresh typically does not require a permit.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Do I need a permit to build a deck in Delaware County, PA?</h3>
                  <p>Most attached decks require a permit, particularly if the deck is elevated above a minimal height threshold or requires new footings. Requirements vary slightly by township.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Who handles the permit process when I hire ProBrothers?</h3>
                  <p>ProBrothers manages the entire permit process for clients: determining requirements during design, preparing and submitting applications and drawings, coordinating any required engineering, and scheduling all required inspections through final sign-off.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Can I do the remodeling work myself to avoid permit requirements?</h3>
                  <p>No. Permit requirements in Pennsylvania apply regardless of who performs the work. Owner-performed work may qualify for certain licensing exemptions in some cases, but the building permit requirement itself still applies.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
